speechtext:
I do not wish to place the minister in an embarrassing position by asking him to decide any legal question this evening. It was not my intention to do that;
I believe that was interjected by other hon. members who have taken part in the discussion. However, since the matter has been brought up, I might suggest to the acting Postmaster General that a royal mail car carries the insignia of His Majesty the King. In my opinion a royal mail car is the property of his majesty; therefore, while there is anything within that car, no person is permitted to enter the car unless he is authorized to do so.
However, I am not going to ask the minister to decide on any legal technicality. I want him to give me the assurance that if I place a three-cent stamp on a letter containing material which might be considered communistic propaganda in the province of Quebec but which does not constitute an infraction of Canadian laws, and mail that letter in a post office, he will undertake to deliver it anywhere in the province of Quebec.
